This directory holds files for testing zstd.NewReader.

Each one is a Zstandard compressed file named as hash.arbitrary-name.zst,
where hash is the first eight hexadecimal digits of the SHA256 hash
of the expected uncompressed content:

	zstd -d < 1890a371.gettysburg.txt-100x.zst | sha256sum | head -c 8
	1890a371

The test uses hash value to verify decompression result.

gettysburg, helloworld and zero-dictionary-ids are those of Go's
internal/zstd. The picture files were made with Python 3.14's
compression.zstd (libzstd 1.5.7), with a checksum, at the level in their
name: the rows of an RGB picture of the size in their name, each sample
the difference from the same sample of the pixel before (as TIFF's
horizontal predictor has them), where pixel (x, y) is

	(x*3 + y*2) & 255, ((x/8)*29 + (y/8)*17 + (x*y)%5) & 255, (x&15)*16 + (y*y/9)%7

512x260 is 399,360 bytes: four blocks, rows that repeat 1,536 bytes on.
